    "summary": "High‑energy Hard To Handle & Good Lovin' with tight jams throughout",
    "blurb": "Features scorching lead on Hard To Handle, a soaring China > Rider transition, and an explosive double‑jam of Good Lovin'. The first set bursts with high‑octane energy while the second set settles into tightly woven improvisations highlighted by Morning Dew and Sing Me Back Home. Excellent soundboard sources capture the performance with crisp clarity.",
    "review": "The Fillmore East on April 25, 1971 finds the Dead in fine form, launching the night with a raucous Hard To Handle that showcases Jerry's blistering lead and Bob's playful, almost ukulele‑like rhythm work. After cruising through classics like Truckin' and Friend Of The Devil, the band lifts into an inspired China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, where Pigpen adds colorful organ swirls that lift the jam to a soaring peak.\nIn the second set the energy shifts into a tightly knit groove: Morning Dew unfurls with heartfelt intensity, Beat It On Down The Line lands crisp and steady, and Good Lovin' erupts twice—first as a tight, high‑energy romp and later as an extended double‑jam where Bill’s drums roar and Phil’s bass drives the flow. The set closes on a warm Sing Me Back Home and a two‑part Not Fade Away medley that keeps the crowd moving, while Uncle John’s Band rounds out the night with a heartfelt encore.",
